{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788304407,"nonce":"YypB7bKfMM1V+OoE+R67lh60G3U=","message":"RULING: THE CASUALTY HALT IS ON THE TRANSITION, NOT ON THE STATE — AND THE ACCEPTANCE SUITE THAT SAID OTHERWISE IS THE THING THAT WAS WRONG. Branch `fix/s21-suite-failures`, commit `4c17009`, off `2bf4539`. Recorded in DESIGN's decisions log. **Not run against a bench; no source in this issue's files changed.**\n\n## The evidence\n\n`accept/61794cd-bleed-triage.py`'s phase 7b, s21 bench, `accept/runs/s21-20260901/suites/61794cd.log`:\n\n    7.5   OK    precondition: there is a downed own-faction colonist to advance past\n    unescaped advance: ok=True reason=ticks ticks=300 error=null\n    7.6a  FAIL  an advance across an own-faction DOWNED colonist does not come back\n                as an ordinary completed advance — it is refused, or it halts (722c951)\n\nFour own-faction colonists were down. An `advance` with NO escapes ran its full 300 ticks and returned normally. The suite read your Acceptance bullet — \"An advance **spanning** an own-faction downing stops at it\" — as a condition on the world.\n\n## Your text pulls both ways in exactly one place, and every specific sentence is a transition\n\n- Scope extension: \"**stop early** when an own-faction pawn goes down or dies **during the advance**, returning what happened **and the tick it happened at**.\" A state has no such tick.\n- Acceptance: \"An advance spanning a **HOSTILE** downing does NOT stop — prove the filter is on faction, **not on the event**.\" A downing is named as an event.\n- Acceptance: \"Replay the M1 shape: an advance **across tick 214,599** stops there instead of running to completion.\"\n- Comment #2: \"Both hooks are on the TRANSITION (`MakeDowned`, `SetDead`), so a pawn already down when an advance starts emits neither — the halt cannot re-fire for the same pawn and cannot wedge.\"\n\nThe implementation matches comment #2 exactly: `JournalHooks.Patch_MakeDowned` and `Patch_SetDead` are Harmony POSTFIXES on `Pawn_HealthTracker.MakeDowned` / `SetDead`. Only the word \"spanning\", read alone, supports the other reading.\n\n## The design argument decides it without appealing to the wording\n\nA state halt wedges a ten-day unattended run, and it does so on a state the run cannot clear. On a bedless map `TakeToBedGate` refuses every candidate and **every** triage verdict is `no-rescuer` (asserted at 6.4a of that suite, banked in `18-triage-downed.json`). There is no act that clears \"a colonist is down and nobody can rescue them\", so every subsequent advance would halt at zero ticks on the same pawn, forever. **That is the identical argument you already made** to keep `bleedout-deadline` firing on `too-slow` and on nothing else: \"where nobody can reach the patient at all, no act clears the condition, the next advance would refuse identically, and a ten-day unattended run stops dead on a state it cannot fix — a wall, not a guard.\"\n\nAnd the escape does not rescue a state halt. `through_casualties` is per-call and deliberately not a mode — your own checks 2.17/2.18 prove it. A state halt would therefore force the escape string onto EVERY advance for the rest of the run, which does not make the guard survivable; it trains the agent to pass it unread. A guard that fires on every call is a guard nobody reads, and the next real casualty gets ridden past with a reason string already in the template.\n\nNothing is lost by staying quiet on the state, because the state is loud elsewhere: `digest.work_coverage` carries it at every glance, `triage` carries the clock, the rescuers and the gate that refused each one, and `pawn.health.ticks_until_bleedout` carries the deadline. The halt exists for the thing that was INVISIBLE — news arriving inside a long advance — which is the M1 failure verbatim (step 148's own `journal_seq:[125,128]` announced Table's downing and the run advanced five more times).\n\n**So the mod is correct as shipped and nothing here changes.** The Acceptance bullet's \"spanning\" is the loose word; every other sentence in this issue and the implementation agree.\n\n## What changed instead: phase 7b now asserts BOTH halves\n\n- **7.5a / 7.5b** — an un-escaped advance made while own-faction colonists are ALREADY down COMPLETES. That is the anti-wedge property, and until now it was only an accident that nothing asserted. It is exactly the observation that produced this failure, turned into a check.\n- **7.6a–7.6h** — a downing armed INSIDE the advance halts it. Staged with **your** `journal-selftest --steps down-at`, because it is the only route that produces a casualty while time is running (it fires from `GameComponentTick`, inside `DoSingleTick`; a `dev:damage` over the protocol lands while paused and proves nothing about an advance). 7.6a keeps the invariant form the phase was written around; 7.6b–7.6f now also assert the SPELLING (`reason:\"casualty\"`, `halted_on.kind/event/pawn_id/player_faction`), which is no longer a guess now that this issue has merged and been bench-proved.\n- **7.7a–7.7e** — the same fixture, the same `until {time.tick \u003e= T}` bound, a second victim, with `through_casualties` on. Same fixture, same span, the escape the only variable — the shape your phases 3 and 4 use for the faction filter. Without it, this suite's module-level escape wrapper was decoration nothing tested.\n- **9.12a** — re-derives the transition claim out of `JournalHooks.cs` offline: both patches present, both `Postfix`. A hook moved onto `Pawn.Downed` fails in `--selftest` instead of on a bench.\n\nThe advance's own bound is a predicate with T read off the game, so \"it stopped early\" is a measurement — your phase 3's argument, borrowed. 7.5a's 300 ticks is the one bare count in the phase and your `pass_time()` note is the justification: the subject under test is the advance, not a state, and the only bleeder left has a ~12,500-tick clock so nothing new can go down inside that window.\n\n## For the orchestrator\n\nRe-run `./accept/61794cd-bleed-triage.py --phase 7` needs phases 0–6 to have built the fixture, so it is a full sweep on a fresh map. **No rebuild is required for this half** — nothing in `TimeDriver.cs`, `JournalHooks.cs` or `JournalVerbs.cs` changed. Phase 7b additionally needs the dev gate on (for `journal-selftest`) and two colonists still standing after phase 7 downs the rest; if either is missing, 7.5a still runs and 7.6/7.7 degrade to a NOTE rather than a false pass.","files":null}]}